39,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The ledger shows that seventy percent of our costs are fixed, regardless of attendance. Additionally, mid-year joiners required manual calculations for their fees, which suggests our current process is inefficient and potentially off-putting for newcomers.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,It appears we are paying a premium for inefficiency. I've discovered that chasing session payments consumes six times the effort of a yearly collection. We must avoid a structure that turns the treasurer's role into a full-time pursuit.,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"My findings confirm a significant imbalance. A small minority of members account for the vast majority of attendance, and those same few individuals are the ones handling all the operational work. It is quite a stark disparity.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,"It's exactly as I suspected. People have started treating their membership like a transaction. The moment we added door charges, the weekday vibe died. Now, members are weighing the cost against the value every single time.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"@Benjamin, that's exactly why we need a solid yearly fee. And @Asuka, since seventy percent of costs are fixed, we can't rely on ""pay-as-you-go."" We need a guaranteed amount coming in, or we're doomed.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, your observation about the disparity in attendance is concerning. If we move toward a more rigid or visible structure, we risk further alienating those who prefer to drift in and out quietly.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, while I sympathize with the ""drifters,"" we cannot let sentimentality blind us to the labor involved. If we implement a complex, tiered system to accommodate everyone, we are simply volunteering the next treasurer for a life of misery.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"I understand the concern for the drifters, but we must also consider the burden on those who are always here. It seems unfair that a few carry both the operational work and the primary use of the club.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, your ""fairness"" is creating a barrier. If we make people feel like they're paying for a service rather than belonging to a club, they'll keep hesitating. We need to stop the transactional mindset.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Shunichi, stop talking about ""mindsets"" and look at the bank statements. People are already struggling to pay on time. If we don't have a clear, guaranteed sum, we can't even afford the hall.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Takumi, I agree that we need a guaranteed sum for the fixed costs. However, we must avoid a system that requires constant manual calculations or makes every member's contribution a point of public scrutiny. Simplicity is key.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, on that we are in complete agreement. Simplicity is not merely a convenience; it is a necessity for the survival of the treasurer. A system that requires constant auditing is a system destined for failure.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"Perhaps a simple two-tier system could work. A base fee to cover the fixed costs, and a separate, modest contribution for those who utilize the facilities most frequently. It would be fair without being overly complex.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, a two-tier system is just a transactional mindset with a different name. You're still asking members to calculate the ""value"" of their visit. Let's just set one flat fee and let people belong.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Shunichi, a flat fee only works if we actually know how much that fee needs to be to cover the bills. We can't just pick a number out of thin air and hope for the best.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Takumi, we have the ledger, so we can calculate that number. However, we must ensure the final fee is inclusive enough that newcomers don't feel they need a manual negotiation just to join us.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"Precisely. Let us avoid any structure that requires a bespoke negotiation for every new member. A streamlined, predictable process is the only way to ensure the treasurer doesn't resign in a fit of despair within three months.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I believe we can achieve both simplicity and fairness. If we set a flat fee that covers our fixed costs, we ensure stability without creating a bureaucratic nightmare for the treasurer.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,"Finally, Jonathan is speaking my language, though I suspect he's just tired of arguing. A single flat fee removes the hesitation. No more calculating value; you're either in the club or you're not. Simple.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Takumi,,"@Jonathan, @Shunichi, a flat fee is fine, but only if it's high enough to cover everything. I saw the bank statements; people are already struggling. We can't afford to be ""inclusive"" if it means we go broke.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
